---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`Carbon and its Compounds | CBSE Class 10 Science | ${provider.name}`}
  description="Complete CBSE Class 10 Chemistry chapter on Carbon and its Compounds. Covers covalent bonding, tetravalency, catenation, homologous series, functional groups, ethanol, ethanoic acid, soap micelle cleansing mechanism, and 20-question quiz."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Carbon &amp; Its Compounds
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Science · Chemistry</span>
      <h1>Carbon and its Compounds</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Comprehensive revision notes on covalent bonding, versatile carbon nature, IUPAC homologous series, reactions of ethanol and ethanoic acid, soap micelles, and a 20-question board quiz.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Key concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Key Concepts at a Glance</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚛️ Bonding in Carbon &amp; Covalent Bond</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Carbon has atomic number 6 (electronic configuration: <strong>2, 4</strong>).</li>
            <li>It cannot gain 4 electrons (forming C⁴⁻ is energetically unfavorable for a nucleus with 6 protons) or lose 4 electrons (requires huge ionization energy).</li>
            <li>Hence, carbon overcomes this problem by <strong>sharing its valence electrons</strong> with other atoms, forming <strong>Covalent Bonds</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Types of Covalent Bonds</strong>:
              <ul>
                <li>Single bond (shares 1 pair of electrons, e.g., H₂, CH₄)</li>
                <li>Double bond (shares 2 pairs of electrons, e.g., O₂, C₂H₄)</li>
                <li>Triple bond (shares 3 pairs of electrons, e.g., N₂, C₂H₂)</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Covalent compounds have low melting and boiling points and are poor conductors of electricity (no free ions).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌟 Versatile Nature of Carbon</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>Millions of carbon compounds exist due to two unique properties:
              <ol>
                <li><strong>Catenation</strong>: The unique ability of carbon atoms to form strong covalent bonds with other carbon atoms, giving rise to long chains, branched chains, and closed rings. C-C bond is exceptionally strong due to carbon's small size.</li>
                <li><strong>Tetravalency</strong>: Having a valency of four, carbon can bond with four other atoms of carbon or other monovalent elements (H, Cl, O, N, S).</li>
              </ol>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Allotropes of Carbon</strong>:
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Diamond</strong>: Rigid 3D tetrahedral structure; hardest substance; non-conductor.</li>
                <li><strong>Graphite</strong>: Hexagonal planar layers bonded by weak van der Waals forces; soft, slippery; excellent conductor due to one free valence electron per carbon.</li>
                <li><strong>Fullerene (C-60)</strong>: Football-shaped cage structure (Buckminsterfullerene).</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧪 Saturated &amp; Unsaturated Hydrocarbons</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Hydrocarbons</strong>: Compounds composed solely of carbon and hydrogen.</li>
            <li><strong>Saturated (Alkanes, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂)</strong>: Carbon atoms linked only by single covalent bonds (e.g., Methane CH₄, Ethane C₂H₆). Burn with a clean blue flame.</li>
            <li><strong>Unsaturated</strong>: Contain double or triple bonds between carbon atoms. Burn with a sooty yellow flame.
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Alkenes (CₙH₂ₙ)</strong>: Contain at least one C=C double bond (e.g., Ethene C₂H₄).</li>
                <li><strong>Alkynes (CₙH₂ₙ₋₂)</strong>: Contain at least one C≡C triple bond (e.g., Ethyne / Acetylene C₂H₂).</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Homologous Series</strong>: A family of carbon compounds having the same functional group and similar chemical properties. Successive members differ by a <strong>−CH₂− unit</strong> and by <strong>14 u molecular mass</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🏷️ Functional Groups &amp; Nomenclature</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>An atom or group of atoms that determines the chemical properties of an organic compound:
              <ul>
                <li><strong>Alcohol (−OH)</strong>: Suffix <em>-ol</em> (e.g., Methanol, Ethanol CH₃CH₂OH)</li>
                <li><strong>Aldehyde (−CHO)</strong>: Suffix <em>-al</em> (e.g., Methanal HCHO, Ethanal CH₃CHO)</li>
                <li><strong>Ketone (−CO−)</strong>: Suffix <em>-one</em> (e.g., Propanone CH₃COCH₃)</li>
                <li><strong>Carboxylic Acid (−COOH)</strong>: Suffix <em>-oic acid</em> (e.g., Ethanoic acid CH₃COOH)</li>
                <li><strong>Haloalkanes (−Cl, −Br)</strong>: Prefix <em>chloro-</em>, <em>bromo-</em> (e.g., Chloromethane CH₃Cl)</li>
              </ul>
            </li>
            <li><strong>Isomerism</strong>: Compounds having the same molecular formula but different structural arrangements (e.g., Butane C₄H₁₀ has n-butane and isobutane).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🔥 Chemical Properties of Carbon Compounds</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Combustion</strong>: Complete oxidation in oxygen releases CO₂, H₂O, heat, and light.
              <br/>CH₄ + 2O₂ ⟶ CO₂ + 2H₂O + Heat &amp; Light</li>
            <li><strong>Oxidation</strong>: Alcohols are oxidized to carboxylic acids using Alkaline KMnO₄ or Acidified K₂Cr₂O₇ (oxidising agents):
              <br/>CH₃CH₂OH ⟶ CH₃COOH</li>
            <li><strong>Addition Reaction</strong>: Unsaturated hydrocarbons add hydrogen in the presence of Nickel/Palladium catalyst to form saturated hydrocarbons (Hydrogenation of vegetable oils).</li>
            <li><strong>Substitution Reaction</strong>: In the presence of sunlight, Chlorine replaces Hydrogen atoms in saturated hydrocarbons one by one:
              <br/>CH₄ + Cl₂ ⟶ CH₃Cl + HCl (sunlight)</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🧼 Ethanol, Ethanoic Acid, Soaps &amp; Detergents</h3>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Ethanol (CH₃CH₂OH)</strong>: Soluble in water in all proportions; reacts with sodium to release hydrogen gas (2Na + 2CH₃CH₂OH ⟶ 2CH₃CH₂ONa + H₂↑).
              <br/>• Dehydration with hot conc. H₂SO₄ (at 443 K) produces Ethene: CH₃CH₂OH ⟶ CH₂=CH₂ + H₂O.</li>
            <li><strong>Ethanoic Acid (Acetic acid - CH₃COOH)</strong>: 5–8% solution in water is <strong>vinegar</strong>. Pure ethanoic acid freezes at 290 K, hence called <strong>glacial acetic acid</strong>.
              <br/>• <strong>Esterification</strong>: Reaction of acid and alcohol gives sweet-smelling <strong>Esters</strong> (used in perfumes):
              <br/>CH₃COOH + C₂H₅OH ⟶ CH₃COOC₂H₅ (Ethyl ethanoate) + H₂O
              <br/>• <strong>Saponification</strong>: Alkaline hydrolysis of ester produces soap.</li>
            <li><strong>Soaps &amp; Micelles</strong>: Sodium/potassium salts of long-chain fatty acids.
              <br/>• <strong>Hydrophobic tail</strong> (hydrocarbon) dissolves in dirt/oil.
              <br/>• <strong>Hydrophilic head</strong> (ionic end) faces outward into water, forming a spherical cluster called a <strong>Micelle</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section" id="visual-carbon">
      <div class="rules-header">
        <span class="eyebrow">Interactive Visual Schematics</span>
